DAY 12 — ACCESS CONTROL & IDOR

Access Control → Controls what a user is allowed to access or do.

Authentication → Who are you?
Authorization → What are you allowed to access?

IDOR = Insecure Direct Object References.

An IDOR occurs when an application exposes an object identifier but fails to verify whether the current user is authorized to access that object.

Example:
GET /profile?id=123

Changing:
id=123 → id=124

is NOT automatically an IDOR. It becomes an IDOR when the server allows access to an unauthorized resource.

BOLA = Broken Object Level Authorization.
Commonly used when discussing similar authorization problems in APIs.

Object identifiers can appear in:

URL paths
Query parameters
JSON bodies
Cookies
Headers

IDOR Testing Workflow:

Find an object identifier.
Send the request to Burp Repeater.
Change ONE identifier.
Send the request.
Compare the responses.
Determine whether authorization was correctly enforced.

Compare:

HTTP status code
Response length
Response body
Response time
Authorization behavior

Core principle:

NEVER rely on hiding IDs for security.
The server must enforce authorization checks.

Example secure logic:

User → Request resource
↓
Server checks: "Is this user authorized for this resource?"
↓
YES → Allow
NO → Deny
